@begin(section)

@title(Get zick)

zick is a Common Lisp program, built with
@link[uri="https://github.com/roswell/roswell"](Roswell) and
@link[uri="https://github.com/ocicl/ocicl"](ocicl).  You build the
@c(zick) executable yourself; there are no prebuilt binaries yet.

@begin(section)
@title(Prerequisites)

@begin(list)
@item(A recent @link[uri="https://www.sbcl.org/"](SBCL) — zick is
developed against SBCL 2.x)
@item(@link[uri="https://github.com/roswell/roswell"](Roswell), for
@c(ros) and @c(ros build))
@item(@link[uri="https://github.com/ocicl/ocicl"](ocicl), for
dependencies)
@end(list)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Building from Source)

Clone the repository:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
git clone https://github.com/djha-skin/zick.git
cd zick
@end(code)

Install the dependencies (this uses the @c(ocicl.csv) manifest in the
repository root):

@begin[lang=sh](code)
ocicl install
@end(code)

Build the executable:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
./scripts/build
@end(code)

The build script bakes the large heap and control-stack sizes zick
needs for big repository resolutions into the binary, so no wrapper or
environment variables are required at run time.  The result is
@c(./com.djhaskin.zick) in the project root.  You can rename it to
@c(zick) and put it on your @c(PATH):

@begin[lang=sh](code)
ln -s "$PWD/com.djhaskin.zick" ~/.local/bin/zick
@end(code)

To use the command names in this manual without a @c(PATH) change, the
examples that follow assume @c(zick) is on your @c(PATH).  (If yours is
still at @c(./com.djhaskin.zick), substitute it in.)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Checking the Installation)

Run zick with no arguments; it prints a short usage summary:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick
@end(code)

And @c(zick help) prints the full CLIFF help page: how options are
gathered, the alias table, and the list of subcommands.

@end(section)

@end(section)
